4. **Correction batch 21 — judge each change against the plans.** Each is a row in the ledger with its measurement, witnesses and recorded limits; read the row before judging.
   - **(a) One authority on whether a caller's documents may still be in a cache** (`CDX-184-r20-01`). `lineage._caller_documents_may_reach` answers "not in `state.sealed`", and every site that turns a cache attribution into a caller obligation asks it; a derived sweep (`_OBLIGATION_SITES` and its guard test) fails if a new obligation site is added without asking it.
   - **(b) A child's own write no longer ends its callers' obligation** (`SELF-184-56`). Amendment 3 §7: "Append possible cohorts; do not treat the last cache write as replacing earlier contents."
   - **(c) A whole-cache removal clears what the cache may hold only where the walk proves it runs** (`SELF-184-57`), with a relative proof: a removal whose leg hands on its Branch's input unchanged has run for every later leg of that Branch.
   - **(d) A re-cache carries where its documents came from** (`SELF-184-59`): a cohort records the caches its documents were retrieved from, so a use off the re-cache owes the cache the documents actually came out of.
   - **(e) A failed path writer owes what a proved one owes** (`SELF-184-58`, `SELF-184-60`), at all three binding-check sites.
   - **(f) Amendment 1 rule 8** — the repeated-run refusal now serves its own code, `PROCESS_IR_CAPABILITY_PROCESS_CALL_REPEATED_RUN_UNSTABLE`, stating its cause and its ways out (`QA-184-s1-r21-01`); a child whose cache writes are unknown is judged by its own required caches, not its caller's vocabulary (`SELF-184-61`); and a per-document child that ends every run with nothing it stored in the cache is exempt, only when the call waits and aborts on error and nothing — no later call, no unwaited call at any depth, no declared outside writer — can write that cache after the removal (`SELF-184-62`, `SELF-184-64`). Judge whether each exemption condition is what rule 8 requires, and whether the served text states it truly.
   - **(g) Call cycles** (`SELF-184-63`): a root nothing can order is seeded with a fail-closed contract, and the unordered set's obligations are iterated to a fixed point, so a supplied cyclic ProcessIR is never weaker evidence than an absent one and an obligation between two cycle members reaches the caller.
   - **(h) Two refusals kept deliberately**, both justified by amendment 3 §7 "Possible opaque/external/child writes introduce unknown possibilities": a second waited call into a child that fills and reads a cache, and a middle that calls a cache writer before a self-filling consumer. Their flattened in-process twins are admitted. Decide whether §7 requires these refusals or whether the batch over-reads it.
